EVEN = [
("Gopal Krishna Gokhale", "Gopal Krishna Gokhale was born on 9 May 1866 in Kotluk, a village in the Ratnagiri district of Maharashtra. He was a gifted teacher of English and mathematics and taught for many years at Fergusson College in Pune. Gokhale was a leader of the moderate group in the Indian National Congress and believed that India's rights could be won through reasoned argument, constitutional methods and steady reform. He served as President of the Congress at its Benares session in 1905. As a member of the Imperial Legislative Council, he criticised the British government's budgets with such careful facts and figures that even his opponents respected him. In 1905, he founded the Servants of India Society to train dedicated workers for national service, education and social reform. He strongly supported free and compulsory primary education. Mahatma Gandhi regarded Gokhale as his political guru and was guided by his advice after returning from South Africa. Gokhale died on 19 February 1915 at the age of forty eight."),
("Shaheed Udham Singh", "Udham Singh was born on 26 December 1899 at Sunam in the Sangrur district of Punjab. He lost both his parents at an early age and grew up in an orphanage in Amritsar. On 13 April 1919, he was present at Jallianwala Bagh when British troops fired on an unarmed crowd, killing hundreds of innocent people. The massacre left a deep mark on his mind, and he vowed to avenge it. He held Michael O'Dwyer, who had been the Lieutenant Governor of Punjab at that time and had supported the firing, responsible for the tragedy. For years, Udham Singh travelled to many countries, working at different jobs and waiting for the right moment. On 13 March 1940, more than twenty years after the massacre, he shot O'Dwyer dead at a meeting in Caxton Hall in London. He made no attempt to escape. At his trial, he called himself Ram Mohammad Singh Azad, a name that reflected the unity of all Indians. He was hanged in London on 31 July 1940."),
("Bahadur Shah Zafar", "Bahadur Shah Zafar was the last Mughal emperor of India. He was born in 1775 and ascended the throne in Delhi in 1837, but by that time real power lay with the East India Company, and his authority hardly extended beyond the Red Fort. Zafar was a gentle and cultured ruler and a fine poet of Urdu, who wrote under the pen name Zafar. When the revolt of 1857 broke out, the soldiers who reached Delhi from Meerut declared the aged emperor the leader of the uprising. His name gave the revolt a symbol of unity, and Hindus and Muslims fought together under his banner. After the British recaptured Delhi in September 1857, Zafar took shelter in Humayun's Tomb, where he was arrested. His sons were shot dead, and he was tried for rebellion and exiled to Rangoon in Burma. He died there in poverty on 7 November 1862. In one of his famous verses, he lamented that he could not even get two yards of land in his beloved homeland for his grave."),
("Pongal", "Pongal is the most important harvest festival of Tamil Nadu and is celebrated for four days in the middle of January, at the beginning of the Tamil month of Thai. It is a festival of thanksgiving to the Sun, to nature and to the cattle that help farmers in the fields. The first day, called Bhogi, is devoted to cleaning homes and discarding old things, which are burnt in a bonfire. The second and main day is Thai Pongal, when families cook freshly harvested rice with milk and jaggery in a clay pot in the open. As the mixture boils over, everyone joyfully shouts Pongalo Pongal, which signifies abundance and prosperity. The third day, Mattu Pongal, honours cows and bullocks, which are bathed, decorated with colourful paint and garlands and fed with sweet pongal. In some villages, the bull taming sport of jallikattu is held. The fourth day, Kaanum Pongal, is for visiting relatives and enjoying outings. Beautiful kolam designs are drawn at the entrance of every house."),
("Bihu, the Festival of Assam", "Bihu is the most loved festival of Assam and is celebrated three times a year, each time linked with a stage of the agricultural cycle. Rongali Bihu, also called Bohag Bihu, comes in the middle of April and marks the Assamese new year and the arrival of spring. It is the most colourful of the three, full of music, feasting and the famous Bihu dance, performed by young men and women to the beat of the dhol and the sound of the pepa, a hornpipe made from buffalo horn. Kongali Bihu, or Kati Bihu, falls in October, when the granaries are nearly empty and the paddy is still growing, so it is observed quietly by lighting earthen lamps in the fields. Bhogali Bihu, or Magh Bihu, is celebrated in January after the harvest. On its eve, people build temporary huts called meji and bhelaghar, enjoy a community feast, and the next morning they burn the meji. Traditional sweets such as pitha and laru are prepared, and people exchange the handwoven gamosa as a mark of respect."),
("Bhagwan Mahavir", "Vardhamana Mahavir was the twenty fourth and last Tirthankara of Jainism. He was born around 599 BCE at Kundagram near Vaishali in present day Bihar, in a royal family. His father, Siddhartha, was the chief of a clan, and his mother, Trishala, was a princess of the Lichchhavis. Although he grew up in comfort, he was deeply drawn to spiritual life. At the age of thirty, he gave up his home and wealth and became an ascetic. For twelve years, he practised severe penance and meditation, and finally he attained kevala jnana, or supreme knowledge, on the bank of the river Rijupalika. After this, he travelled for about thirty years, teaching people in the language of the common people. He preached five great vows: non violence, truthfulness, non stealing, chastity and non possession. He laid special stress on ahimsa towards every living being. He attained nirvana at Pawapuri at the age of seventy two. Mahavir Jayanti is celebrated in the month of Chaitra with great devotion."),
("Goswami Tulsidas", "Goswami Tulsidas was one of the greatest poets of Hindi literature. Tradition places his birth in the sixteenth century at Rajapur, on the bank of the Yamuna, in the present day Chitrakoot district of Uttar Pradesh. According to tradition, he lost his parents early and spent a difficult childhood before he was taken in by his guru, Narharidas, who taught him the story of Lord Rama. His most famous work is the Ramcharitmanas, which retells the Ramayana in the simple and sweet Awadhi language. Because it was written in the language of the people, the epic reached every village of north India and is still read and sung in homes and temples. The Ramlila performances held during Dussehra are largely based on it. Tulsidas also wrote the Hanuman Chalisa, Vinay Patrika, Kavitavali and Dohavali. His works teach devotion, duty, humility and respect for family and society. He spent his last years in Varanasi and passed away in 1623 near the Assi Ghat on the bank of the Ganga."),
("Mirabai", "Mirabai was a Rajput princess and one of the most famous saint poets of the Bhakti movement. She was born in the early sixteenth century at Kudki in the Merta region of Rajasthan. From her childhood, she regarded Lord Krishna as her eternal beloved and devoted her whole life to him. She was married to Bhoj Raj, the crown prince of Mewar, but after his early death she refused to follow many customs expected of a royal widow. Instead, she sang and danced in temples in praise of Krishna and mixed freely with saints and common people. Her behaviour angered some members of the royal family, who are said to have tried to harm her in many ways, but she remained firm in her faith. Mirabai's padas, written in Rajasthani and Braj, are full of love, longing and surrender to God. Songs such as Payoji maine Ram ratan dhan payo are sung even today. She spent her last years in Dwarka, where, according to legend, she merged into the idol of Krishna."),
("Chandragupta Maurya", "Chandragupta Maurya was the founder of the Maurya empire, the first empire to unite most of the Indian subcontinent. With the guidance of his teacher and adviser, Chanakya, also known as Kautilya, he overthrew the unpopular Nanda dynasty of Magadha around 321 BCE and made Pataliputra his capital. He then extended his rule to the northwest after the departure of Alexander's forces. Around 305 BCE, he defeated Seleucus Nicator, the Greek ruler of the region, who gave him large territories in the northwest in a treaty. Seleucus also sent his ambassador, Megasthenes, to the Maurya court. In his book Indica, Megasthenes described the well planned city of Pataliputra, its wooden walls and the efficient administration of the empire. Chanakya's Arthashastra gives a detailed picture of statecraft, taxation and espionage in this period. According to Jain tradition, Chandragupta gave up his throne in his later years, became a Jain monk and ended his life by fasting at Shravanabelagola in Karnataka."),
("Nalanda University", "Nalanda was one of the greatest centres of learning in the ancient world. It was founded in the fifth century CE by Kumaragupta I of the Gupta dynasty, near Rajgir in present day Bihar, and it flourished for nearly seven hundred years. At its peak, it is said to have had about ten thousand students and two thousand teachers, who came from India as well as from China, Korea, Japan, Tibet, Indonesia and Central Asia. Students had to pass a difficult oral test at the gate before they were admitted. Subjects taught included Buddhist philosophy, the Vedas, logic, grammar, medicine, mathematics and astronomy. The famous Chinese traveller Xuanzang studied and taught at Nalanda in the seventh century and left a detailed account of its life. Its huge library, known as Dharmaganja, had three large buildings. Around 1193, Nalanda was attacked and destroyed by Bakhtiyar Khilji, and the library is said to have burned for months. The ruins were declared a World Heritage Site in 2016, and a new Nalanda University now functions at Rajgir."),
("The Konark Sun Temple", "The Sun Temple at Konark, on the coast of Odisha, is one of the finest examples of Indian temple architecture. It was built in the thirteenth century by King Narasimhadeva I of the Eastern Ganga dynasty and is dedicated to Surya, the Sun God. The entire temple is designed in the form of a gigantic chariot of the Sun, with twenty four beautifully carved stone wheels pulled by seven horses. The wheels are not merely decorative, since their spokes cast shadows that can be used to tell the time of day, and they also represent the hours and the months of the year. Every surface of the temple is covered with carvings of gods, dancers, musicians, animals and scenes of daily life. European sailors once called it the Black Pagoda and used it as a landmark for navigation. Although the main tower has collapsed, the remaining structure is still magnificent. The temple was declared a World Heritage Site in 1984, and a Konark wheel appears on the back of the ten rupee note."),
("Pandit Madan Mohan Malaviya", "Pandit Madan Mohan Malaviya was born on 25 December 1861 in Allahabad. He was a teacher, lawyer, journalist, social reformer and one of the leading figures of the freedom movement. He served as President of the Indian National Congress four times. His greatest contribution was to education. With the support of people from every part of the country, including rulers of princely states and ordinary citizens, he founded the Banaras Hindu University in 1916. He wanted it to be an institution that combined the best of Indian culture with modern science and technology. Today it is one of the largest residential universities in Asia. Malaviya ji also worked to remove untouchability, promoted the Hindi language in Devanagari script and helped to popularise the motto Satyameva Jayate. He edited several newspapers and was known for his simplicity and gentle manner, which earned him the title of Mahamana. He passed away on 12 November 1946, and he was awarded the Bharat Ratna posthumously in 2015."),
("Acharya Vinoba Bhave", "Vinoba Bhave was born on 11 September 1895 in the village of Gagode in Maharashtra. He was a scholar, a thinker and one of the closest followers of Mahatma Gandhi. In 1940, Gandhi chose him as the first individual satyagrahi to protest against India being dragged into the Second World War. Vinoba is best known for the Bhoodan movement. On 18 April 1951, at Pochampalli village in Telangana, landless families told him they had no land to cultivate. A landlord in the meeting offered one hundred acres of his land for them. This incident inspired Vinoba to walk across the country, appealing to landowners to donate a part of their land to the landless. Over the next years, he travelled thousands of kilometres on foot, and lakhs of acres of land were donated. The movement later expanded into Gramdan, in which entire villages pooled their land. In 1958, he became the first person to receive the Ramon Magsaysay Award. He died on 15 November 1982 at Paunar, and he was awarded the Bharat Ratna in 1983."),
("The Chipko Movement", "The Chipko movement was a famous people's movement to protect forests in the Himalayan region of present day Uttarakhand. In the early 1970s, the government allowed contractors to cut trees in the hills, while local villagers, who depended on the forests for fodder, fuel and water, were denied even small amounts of wood. In 1973, villagers of Mandal in the Chamoli district, led by Chandi Prasad Bhatt and his organisation, decided to stop the felling by hugging the trees. The word chipko means to cling. The most famous episode took place in March 1974 at Reni village, where the men were away. Gaura Devi led a group of women who stood in front of the loggers and refused to move, and the contractors had to go back. Sunderlal Bahuguna carried the message of the movement across the country through long padyatras. As a result, in 1980 the government imposed a fifteen year ban on cutting green trees in the Himalayan forests of the state. The movement showed the power of ordinary women in protecting the environment."),
("Sir C V Raman", "Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman was born on 7 November 1888 in Tiruchirappalli in Tamil Nadu. He was a brilliant student and completed his master's degree in physics at a very young age. Because there were few opportunities for research in India at that time, he joined the government finance department, but he continued his experiments in the evenings at the Indian Association for the Cultivation of Science in Calcutta. On 28 February 1928, he discovered that when light passes through a transparent substance, a small part of it is scattered with a change in its wavelength. This discovery came to be known as the Raman effect, and it became a powerful tool for studying the structure of molecules. The Nobel Prize in Physics was awarded to him in 1930, making him the first scientist from Asia to receive it in that subject. He later founded the Raman Research Institute in Bangalore. He received the Bharat Ratna in 1954. National Science Day is celebrated every year on 28 February to mark his discovery. He passed away on 21 November 1970."),
]
